1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,666
What if a robot arrived with the wrong physics model? Sol Zero is a
simulated robot lab

2
00:00:06,666 --> 00:00:13,333
It gets twelve experiments to learn from measurements, then must hit
five untouched targets, one shot each

3
00:00:13,333 --> 00:00:20,000
This is a recorded development run, replayed from its actual
decisions.

4
00:00:20,000 --> 00:00:22,857
Open source Omnigent connects five specialist roles

5
00:00:22,857 --> 00:00:25,714
The Theorist proposes rival laws

6
00:00:25,714 --> 00:00:28,571
The Experimentalist compares candidate tests

7
00:00:28,571 --> 00:00:31,428
The Operator makes the measurement

8
00:00:31,428 --> 00:00:34,285
The Analyst judges the evidence

9
00:00:34,285 --> 00:00:37,142
The principal investigator decides when to stop

10
00:00:37,142 --> 00:00:40,000
Agents exchange structured records and use tools, rather than
receiving a scripted discovery.

11
00:00:40,000 --> 00:00:45,000
Here is a concrete failure from the ledger

12
00:00:45,000 --> 00:00:50,000
Before the second experiment, one initial law predicted a range of two
point three six metres

13
00:00:50,000 --> 00:00:55,000
The measurement was only zero point five six three metres

14
00:00:55,000 --> 00:01:00,000
The Analyst rejected that law, recorded its uncertainty assessment,
and considered confounding explanations.

15
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:04,000
The lab does not simply repeat a fixed sequence

16
00:01:04,000 --> 00:01:08,000
The ledger records a tentative follow up, then compares it with the
next chosen test

17
00:01:08,000 --> 00:01:12,000
In this run, a heavy sample drop became a light sample launch

18
00:01:12,000 --> 00:01:16,000
Six decisions changed

19
00:01:16,000 --> 00:01:20,000
After ten experiments, the investigator chose to commit.

20
00:01:20,000 --> 00:01:24,000
The committed firing table produced five hits out of five

21
00:01:24,000 --> 00:01:28,000
Three targets were beyond the experimental launch range

22
00:01:28,000 --> 00:01:32,000
These miss distances come from the server score for this same session

23
00:01:32,000 --> 00:01:36,000
The animation replays the recorded shots and their graded landing
points

24
00:01:36,000 --> 00:01:40,000
It does not generate replacement results.

25
00:01:40,000 --> 00:01:45,000
The result is a working, auditable scientific loop, with a predictive
only claim

26
00:01:45,000 --> 00:01:50,000
The actual run took about seventeen active minutes

27
00:01:50,000 --> 00:01:55,000
This development example does not prove superiority over random tests
or a single agent

28
00:01:55,000 --> 00:02:00,000
Our next experiment is a matched comparison, using the same tools,
worlds, and budgets.
